I'm here to tell you the story of the  accidental discovery of artificial dyes  the William Henry Perkin discovered  movin in 1856 he was trying to make  Quinny  instead of making Quin een Perkin ended  up with a black powder but he didn't  throw the black powder away he  experimented on it and found that he  could use it to dye fabric purple here  we have a sample of fabric dyed with  moving that was used to make a dress  worn by Queen Victoria in 1860 after  Perkin made movie a kaleidoscope of  artificial dives began to appear on the  markets however as with many new  scientific developments there were  teething problems these artificial dyes  would fade easily as can be seen here in  this sample of cotton fabric that was  dyed with moving in 1860 you can see the  spots where the dye has faded the  chemical structure of the artificial  dyes was fairly unstable and the dyed  material would fade in the sunlight  James Morton had inherited a family  textile company to test how well dyed  fabric lasted Under the Sun James Morton  sent some samples of fabric out to his  brother-in-law who lived in India  Morton instructed his brother-in-law to  leave samples of different fabrics on  the roof of his house in the full glare  of sun lights what Morton found in these  experiments is that some dyes lasted  better under the Sun than others so here  you can see a selection of dyes that  lasted quite well under the Sun but he  found that some dyes and here we've got  a sample of his competitors dyes they  didn't last very well under the Sun at  all in fact you can barely recognize  them so Morton looked at which dyes  lasted better when placed in full  sunlight and got his chemist John  Christy to make dyes based on those  results  [Music]  Morton marketed his dives to high-end  fashion chains like Burberry's and  Liberty's he marketed them based on the  fact that his dyes would not fade and  here we see an advert placed by burr  breweries in the Queens magazine the  queen magazine was quite a high-end  magazine and you can see that they're  advertising dyed fabrics where the dyes  are indelible they won't fade in  sunlight you can even see a reference  here to the experiments that James  Morton made in India from Perkins  accidental discovery of moving to  Morton's mission to find dyes that would  not fade the objects here tell the story  of the relationship between chemistry  and fashion subscribe to the science  museum channel to watch more stories  from the stores and discover other  fascinating science stories
 

